WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:24.440
I remember this album on our parlor table at home.

00:24.440 --> 00:28.880
I remember the old pictures from Norway that Mama and Papa brought with them when they

00:28.880 --> 00:34.200
came to this country, the uncles and the aunts and the cousins I had there.

00:34.200 --> 00:36.680
And I remember my family.

00:36.680 --> 00:47.560
I remember my sister Dagmar, and my brother Nell, and of course Papa.

00:47.560 --> 00:54.280
But most of all, when I look through this album, most of all I remember Mama.

00:54.280 --> 00:57.520
Yes, here's Mama.

00:57.520 --> 01:02.240
Brought to you by Maxwell House Coffee, the richer, mellower blend.

01:02.240 --> 01:08.800
Already a favorite in Mama's time, today more people buy and enjoy Maxwell House than any

01:08.800 --> 01:11.280
other brand at any price.

01:11.280 --> 01:16.840
Today as always, this sign of good coffee means Maxwell House.

01:16.840 --> 01:34.880
The coffee that's always good to the last drop.

01:34.880 --> 01:36.680
Let me see if you are boiling over yet.

01:36.680 --> 01:39.680
Oh, it's nothing to joke about, Mama.

01:39.680 --> 01:40.680
I feel awful.

01:40.680 --> 01:43.200
Obvious when you have the spelling test you feel awful.

01:43.200 --> 01:44.200
But this has nothing to do with the test, Mama.

01:44.200 --> 01:45.200
Yeah.

01:45.200 --> 01:46.200
I just feel awful.

01:46.200 --> 01:47.200
I just don't feel well.

01:47.200 --> 01:48.200
Well, you have no fever.

01:48.200 --> 01:53.200
Oh, Mama, that can't reach far down enough to show how bad I feel.

01:53.200 --> 01:54.200
Oh, stomachache.

01:54.200 --> 01:55.200
Awful.

01:55.200 --> 01:56.200
You will have to try castor oil.

01:56.200 --> 01:57.200
Oh, no.

01:57.200 --> 01:58.200
That is the best for stomachache.

01:58.200 --> 01:59.200
Well, this isn't a regular kind of a stomachache, Mama.

01:59.200 --> 02:00.200
It kind of moves all around.

02:00.200 --> 02:01.200
One minute it's a stomachache, and then it's a headache.

02:01.200 --> 02:02.200
And then it is a spelling test.

02:02.200 --> 02:03.200
Honest, Mama.

02:03.200 --> 02:04.200
I feel sick.

02:04.200 --> 02:05.200
I have been called, Doctor.

02:05.200 --> 02:06.200
The doctor has been called.

02:06.200 --> 02:07.200
I have been called.

02:07.200 --> 02:08.200
I have been called.

02:08.200 --> 02:09.200
I have been called.

02:09.200 --> 02:10.200
I have been called.

02:10.200 --> 02:11.200
I have been called.

02:11.200 --> 02:12.200
I have been called.

02:12.200 --> 02:13.200
I have been called.

02:13.200 --> 02:14.200
I have been called.

02:14.200 --> 02:16.200
I have been called, Doctor Johnson, then.

02:16.200 --> 02:17.200
Oh, no, Mama.

02:17.200 --> 02:19.200
Oh, well, if you feel sick.

02:19.200 --> 02:21.200
But she's so expensive.

02:21.200 --> 02:25.200
Besides, I don't think she's a very good doctor anymore.

02:25.200 --> 02:29.200
You were just saying that because Doctor Johnson is on to you.

02:29.200 --> 02:32.200
Remember what he said last week?

02:32.200 --> 02:38.200
There is nothing wrong with this child that the burning down of the school would not cure.

02:38.200 --> 02:39.200
Remember?

02:39.200 --> 02:40.200
Remember?

02:40.200 --> 02:42.200
Stop it, Mama.

02:42.200 --> 02:44.200
No, you were very sick.

02:44.200 --> 02:45.200
Oh, yeah.

02:45.200 --> 02:46.200
Well, I am.

02:46.200 --> 02:49.200
Well, you will feel better when the test is over.

02:49.200 --> 02:51.200
Do I have to go to school, Mama?

02:51.200 --> 02:52.200
Yeah.

02:52.200 --> 02:56.200
Well, don't blame me if something terrible happens to me.

02:56.200 --> 02:58.200
Oh, stop it, Dagmar.

02:58.200 --> 03:00.200
But it's got into you lately.

03:00.200 --> 03:07.200
Every time you do not feel like doing something, you make a long face and you say you are sick.

03:07.200 --> 03:08.200
Well, please.

03:08.200 --> 03:09.200
You are not.

03:09.200 --> 03:10.200
Now, run along to school.

03:10.200 --> 03:11.200
You'll be late.

03:11.200 --> 03:12.200
Fly.

03:12.200 --> 03:14.200
Oh, Dagmar, Tommy Jordan was looking all over for you.

03:14.200 --> 03:15.200
For me?

03:15.200 --> 03:18.200
His father bought him a whole box of all-day suckers and he wanted to give you one.

03:18.200 --> 03:19.200
Oh, boy, where'd he go?

03:19.200 --> 03:21.200
Oh, I guess he was just out front.

03:21.200 --> 03:23.200
Hey, Tommy, one for me.

03:23.200 --> 03:25.200
She is very sick.

03:25.200 --> 03:27.200
I should have been starting that again.

03:27.200 --> 03:28.200
Mm, spelling test.

03:28.200 --> 03:29.200
Did you forget something?

03:29.200 --> 03:32.200
Oh, I just wanted to tell you that I'm going to Grace's after school this afternoon, Mama.

03:32.200 --> 03:33.200
That is, if you don't mind.

03:33.200 --> 03:35.200
I thought about your practicing.

03:35.200 --> 03:36.200
Well, I can do that tonight.

03:36.200 --> 03:38.200
You have your homework tonight?

03:38.200 --> 03:42.200
Oh, Mama, what difference does it make if I mispractice once in a while?

03:42.200 --> 03:43.200
Once in a while?

03:43.200 --> 03:47.200
Yesterday and the day before and ten minutes the day before that.

03:47.200 --> 03:50.200
That is not what Papa pays for Mrs. Kleiner.

03:50.200 --> 03:51.200
I hate music lessons.

03:51.200 --> 03:53.200
Well, you were the one who wanted to take them.

03:53.200 --> 03:56.200
But Mrs. Kleiner makes everything so hard.

03:56.200 --> 03:59.200
Everybody wants something for nothing in this house.

03:59.200 --> 04:02.200
Oh, Mama, please let me go to Grace's this afternoon.

04:02.200 --> 04:05.200
You have to practice, don't you?

04:05.200 --> 04:09.200
Now I know why they say all musicians have a secret sorrow.

04:09.200 --> 04:12.200
They were made to practice when they were young.

04:12.200 --> 04:14.200
Oh, Papa's going to fix that, Mama.

04:14.200 --> 04:17.200
He was going to fix it two weeks ago, and it is still stuck.

04:17.200 --> 04:19.200
You know what we were going to do this afternoon at Grace's, Mama?

04:19.200 --> 04:21.200
Wood burning.

04:21.200 --> 04:22.200
I wonder what the heck was up with.

04:22.200 --> 04:23.200
Oh, anything, Mama.

04:23.200 --> 04:24.200
It doesn't matter.

04:24.200 --> 04:25.200
Grace got a wood burning set for Christmas.

04:25.200 --> 04:27.200
It's such fun.

04:27.200 --> 04:31.200
Why do I always have to make you do everything, Ketchen?

04:31.200 --> 04:32.200
You have to practice.

04:32.200 --> 04:33.200
I know it.

04:33.200 --> 04:34.200
I know it.

04:34.200 --> 04:37.200
And do you not think it would be a good idea to go to school?

04:37.200 --> 04:38.200
Look at the time.

04:38.200 --> 04:39.200
Call me, Mama.

04:39.200 --> 04:40.200
I'm going to be late.

04:40.200 --> 04:41.200
Why don't you tell me?

04:41.200 --> 04:42.200
Yes, tell me.

04:55.200 --> 04:56.200
Nathan.

04:56.200 --> 04:58.200
You surprised me here, Nathan.

04:58.200 --> 05:01.200
I made a little lunch outside, and they gave me those.

05:01.200 --> 05:03.200
I thought I'd play a little yoke on you.

05:03.200 --> 05:05.200
You nearly closed the door.

05:05.200 --> 05:07.200
Now, who are they from?

05:07.200 --> 05:08.200
Bears.

05:08.200 --> 05:12.200
Well, I thought maybe, Martha, that we could go to market together.

05:12.200 --> 05:14.200
No, no, I have not made my bed yet.

05:14.200 --> 05:15.200
Oh, Martha.

05:15.200 --> 05:18.200
Do you know what Mrs. Yantrout told me?

05:18.200 --> 05:19.200
What?

05:19.200 --> 05:22.200
Someday Mrs. Lindquist does not make her beds until 3 o'clock.

05:22.200 --> 05:24.200
Oh, I could not live like that.

05:24.200 --> 05:26.200
Oh, that would make me feel like a pig.

05:26.200 --> 05:27.200
Make yourself at home, Annie.

05:27.200 --> 05:28.200
I will be with you in a yiffy.

05:28.200 --> 05:29.200
Well, where are you going?

05:29.200 --> 05:30.200
My bed.

05:30.200 --> 05:32.200
Oh, then I will help you.

05:32.200 --> 05:33.200
I can make my own bed.

05:33.200 --> 05:34.200
Oh, no, no.

05:34.200 --> 05:36.200
Four hands is better than two.

05:36.200 --> 05:37.200
I will help you.

05:37.200 --> 05:39.200
In that way, it will go quicker.

05:41.200 --> 05:44.200
Well, since men have the best bed in the kitchen.

05:44.200 --> 05:46.200
Oh, well, I would bid you right away.

05:46.200 --> 05:49.200
I forgot I left the coffee on the stove.

05:49.200 --> 05:52.200
Oh, I am glad I have got no boys.

05:52.200 --> 05:54.200
They are worse than pigs.

05:54.200 --> 05:58.200
All day long I am asking Alice to pick up this room and look.

05:58.200 --> 06:01.200
Well, anyway, you do not leave it until 3 o'clock.

06:01.200 --> 06:03.200
Someday that would like to.

06:03.200 --> 06:05.200
Oh, you, Martha.

06:05.200 --> 06:10.200
Yes, sometimes I get so sick of bed sheets and bed sheets.

06:10.200 --> 06:12.200
I do not like it.

06:12.200 --> 06:14.200
I do not like it.

06:14.200 --> 06:16.200
I do not like it.

06:16.200 --> 06:20.200
I get so sick of bed sheets and dirty clothes.

06:20.200 --> 06:22.200
I wish we got Eskimos.

06:22.200 --> 06:25.200
I did not need to do such things.

06:25.200 --> 06:26.200
Here, put it down.

06:26.200 --> 06:31.200
You can thank your lucky stars that you are not Mrs. Edlund.

06:31.200 --> 06:32.200
Why?

06:32.200 --> 06:37.200
Because now she has one more bed to make than she used to.

06:37.200 --> 06:38.200
What do you mean?

06:38.200 --> 06:43.200
Then on the 30th anniversary, Mr. Edlund gave her those newfangled twin beds.

06:43.200 --> 06:45.200
You know, with the spaces.

06:45.200 --> 06:48.200
Yes, Mrs. Johnson told me.

06:48.200 --> 06:51.200
And what are you going to have for supper?

06:51.200 --> 06:53.200
Oh, Martha, I have not made up my mind.

06:53.200 --> 06:54.200
You too?

06:54.200 --> 06:59.200
Well, I was thinking of having pins, knuckles and sauerkraut, but I am not sure yet.

06:59.200 --> 07:01.200
That would not be right for the children.

07:01.200 --> 07:03.200
Well, everything is so expensive.

07:03.200 --> 07:06.200
A round steak is 14 cents a pound.

07:06.200 --> 07:08.200
I would not pay that.

07:08.200 --> 07:09.200
But I have to think of something.

07:09.200 --> 07:12.200
Every day, every day.

07:12.200 --> 07:15.200
Martha, where does this letter go?

07:15.200 --> 07:21.200
Oh, I wonder what else he has in his pocket.

07:21.200 --> 07:27.200
If I knew what boys carried around in their pockets, I would know more than Mr. Edison.

07:27.200 --> 07:29.200
Oh, Martha.

07:29.200 --> 07:30.200
What?

07:30.200 --> 07:33.200
Martha, look.

07:33.200 --> 07:35.200
Cigarettes.

07:35.200 --> 07:39.200
Bill, aren't you going to say something?

07:39.200 --> 07:41.200
What can I say?

07:41.200 --> 07:47.200
If I had the boy as young as Nell, and he was going around smoking, I would have had something to say.

07:47.200 --> 07:50.200
I would put him over my knee and I would thank him, I would.

07:50.200 --> 07:51.200
Nell is 16 years old.

07:51.200 --> 07:53.200
Well, then let's not thank him.

07:53.200 --> 07:55.200
16 is too young to smoke.

07:55.200 --> 07:56.200
I know.

07:56.200 --> 07:58.200
I wish I did not.

07:58.200 --> 08:00.200
Well, then why do you let him?

08:00.200 --> 08:03.200
Why do I have to make everybody do everything?

08:03.200 --> 08:06.200
Then, Martha, you cannot let him go around smoking.

08:06.200 --> 08:12.200
You will stunt his growth and maybe give him the long term.

08:12.200 --> 08:14.200
All right, Martha, now come along.

08:14.200 --> 08:16.200
Let's go to store.

08:16.200 --> 08:20.200
Why do we always have to eat every day?

08:20.200 --> 08:24.200
I'll tell you something, Annie.

08:24.200 --> 08:26.200
Sometimes I wish I was an old maid.

08:26.200 --> 08:29.200
Oh, Martha.

08:29.200 --> 08:30.200
What does a family mean?

08:30.200 --> 08:31.200
Work.

08:31.200 --> 08:33.200
And who appreciates it?

08:33.200 --> 08:37.200
Nobody.

08:37.200 --> 08:39.200
This is just my bad day.

08:39.200 --> 08:45.200
Come along.

08:45.200 --> 08:49.200
So my man said to me, where did you ever hear a story like that?

08:49.200 --> 08:50.200
And what did you say?

08:50.200 --> 08:52.200
I say from Yenny Oberg, I say.

08:52.200 --> 08:54.200
And what did he say?

08:54.200 --> 08:55.200
Yenny Oberg.

08:55.200 --> 08:58.200
Why, do you pay any attention to it, then?

08:58.200 --> 09:01.200
You know what a gossip and lout about Yenny Oberg is.

09:01.200 --> 09:04.200
Gossip and lout, Martha said.

09:04.200 --> 09:08.200
I laughed until I had such pain here.

09:08.200 --> 09:10.200
What is the matter?

09:10.200 --> 09:14.200
Never seem to fail to talk of somebody and better they are.

09:14.200 --> 09:15.200
Yenny Oberg.

09:15.200 --> 09:19.200
Oh, hello, Mrs. Oberg.

09:19.200 --> 09:21.200
I see why you are speaking of me.

09:21.200 --> 09:22.200
Mrs. Oberg.

09:22.200 --> 09:23.200
Mrs. Oberg.

09:23.200 --> 09:26.200
I was going to call you both later this morning.

09:26.200 --> 09:27.200
Oh, yeah?

09:27.200 --> 09:31.200
Yeah, my man goes to lunch this evening.

09:31.200 --> 09:33.200
I was going to have a few of the girls in.

09:33.200 --> 09:34.200
Oh, that would be nice.

09:34.200 --> 09:36.200
We would play romney, can you all come?

09:36.200 --> 09:38.200
Oh, yeah, that would be fine.

09:38.200 --> 09:39.200
I would love to, Mrs. Oberg.

09:39.200 --> 09:41.200
Well, how about you, Mrs. Hanson?

09:41.200 --> 09:43.200
I am not much of a hand for cards.

09:43.200 --> 09:44.200
Oh, what is the difference?

09:44.200 --> 09:46.200
We play for fun only.

09:46.200 --> 09:49.200
Well, it is nice of you to ask me, but I have too much to do at home.

09:49.200 --> 09:51.200
Oh, now, Martha, what have you got to do?

09:51.200 --> 09:56.200
Well, I have the sewing and I have to get nails at the geometry and

09:56.200 --> 10:00.200
then there is the drawer in the kitchen that must be fixed.

10:00.200 --> 10:02.200
Oh, they will at last fix that.

10:02.200 --> 10:03.200
Are you crazy?

10:03.200 --> 10:06.200
Well, he will not start it unless I am there to push him on.

10:06.200 --> 10:08.200
Oh, he is just like my man.

10:08.200 --> 10:09.200
Mine too.

10:09.200 --> 10:12.200
These tomatoes are not very fresh.

10:12.200 --> 10:16.200
Well, if you had been squeezed and pinched and dropped as much as that tomato is,

10:16.200 --> 10:18.200
you wouldn't be very fresh either.

10:18.200 --> 10:19.200
How much are they?

10:19.200 --> 10:21.200
Fifteen cents a quart.

10:21.200 --> 10:22.200
That is highly robbery.

10:22.200 --> 10:24.200
I wish you would come, Mrs. Hanson.

10:24.200 --> 10:28.200
Martha, let your family look out for themselves for this month.

10:28.200 --> 10:31.200
They don't appreciate what we do for them anyway, do they, girls?

10:31.200 --> 10:32.200
They certainly do not.

10:32.200 --> 10:36.200
Then only this morning, Martha, you said you wished you were an old maid.

10:36.200 --> 10:41.200
Oh, yes, we wish you would come, Mrs. Hanson.

10:41.200 --> 10:44.200
Then I would like to, but the sewing just must be done.

10:44.200 --> 10:46.200
Oh, she is hopeless.

10:46.200 --> 10:49.200
All she wants to be is used to hire girls for her family.

10:49.200 --> 10:53.200
Well, if you change your mind, we would want you, you remember.

10:53.200 --> 10:55.200
I will, thank you.

10:55.200 --> 10:57.200
Everything is so dear.

10:57.200 --> 10:59.200
I wish I knew what to get for supper.

10:59.200 --> 11:01.200
We are having that trouble too.

11:01.200 --> 11:02.200
There is a heck with it.

11:02.200 --> 11:04.200
I'm going to have the sauerkraut.

11:04.200 --> 11:06.200
I think maybe stew.

11:06.200 --> 11:07.200
My man likes that.

11:07.200 --> 11:09.200
He only had stew two nights ago.

11:09.200 --> 11:11.200
But did you see the chops today?

11:11.200 --> 11:13.200
We had chops last night.

11:13.200 --> 11:14.200
You like fish?

11:14.200 --> 11:16.200
I do not know what I want.

11:16.200 --> 11:23.200
My sister-in-law in the inner circle sent me the most wonderful recipe for meatloaf.

11:23.200 --> 11:25.200
Meatloaf with the knife.

11:25.200 --> 11:26.200
Oh, but this is wonderful.

11:26.200 --> 11:28.200
My man had four headings.

11:28.200 --> 11:29.200
Yes.

11:29.200 --> 11:31.200
And it takes five hours to make.

11:31.200 --> 11:32.200
Why is it so hard?

11:32.200 --> 11:35.200
Because everything must be just so.

11:35.200 --> 11:37.200
But that is what makes it so good.

11:37.200 --> 11:41.200
Then maybe you would be so kind as to give me this recipe, Mrs. Lindquist.

11:41.200 --> 11:44.200
You are going to spend five hours making supper for your family?

11:44.200 --> 11:47.200
Now I know she is crazy.

11:51.200 --> 11:56.200
I will always remember how hard I practiced that afternoon.

11:56.200 --> 12:03.200
Oh, this is the way you practice?

12:03.200 --> 12:10.200
Oh, this magazine just happened to be on the piano, Mama.

12:10.200 --> 12:11.200
So?

12:11.200 --> 12:17.200
Oh, well, I can do these even when I do read, Mama.

12:17.200 --> 12:18.200
Listen.

12:18.200 --> 12:27.200
This is the magazine of A, Catherine.

12:27.200 --> 12:30.200
You know what Mrs. Kleiner said last time?

12:30.200 --> 12:31.200
You are not working hard enough.

12:31.200 --> 12:34.200
Oh, she wants me to practice all the time.

12:34.200 --> 12:37.200
You have been practicing exactly five minutes.

12:37.200 --> 12:38.200
Oh, it must be more than that, Mama.

12:38.200 --> 12:39.200
Uncle, look.

12:39.200 --> 12:40.200
Now you sit down.

12:40.200 --> 12:41.200
And you practice.

12:41.200 --> 12:48.200
This is the greatest moment of my life.

12:48.200 --> 12:49.200
I smell it, Mama.

12:49.200 --> 12:51.200
I got a hundred gold stars.

12:51.200 --> 12:54.200
Oh, a hundred and a gold star.

12:54.200 --> 12:56.200
Oh, thanks, Mama.

12:56.200 --> 12:58.200
That is wonderful.

12:58.200 --> 13:01.200
Now I can do this with the only other woman who is perfect.

13:01.200 --> 13:03.200
I bet you feel better now.

13:03.200 --> 13:04.200
Oh, yes, Mama.

13:04.200 --> 13:06.200
And now I am going over to that end.

13:06.200 --> 13:07.200
We are going to play a song.

13:07.200 --> 13:08.200
Oh, which reminds me.

13:08.200 --> 13:10.200
I need a bunch of parsley.

13:10.200 --> 13:11.200
Oh, I am sorry.

13:11.200 --> 13:13.200
But we are going to play a dry good song.

13:13.200 --> 13:15.200
Oh, but before you open your song,

13:15.200 --> 13:17.200
will you run down to Mr. Bull's

13:17.200 --> 13:20.200
and get him to give you a nice bunch of parsley?

13:20.200 --> 13:22.200
But Sarah Ann is expecting me right away, Mama.

13:22.200 --> 13:24.200
I need the parsley for supper.

13:24.200 --> 13:25.200
Couldn't Catherine get it?

13:25.200 --> 13:26.200
Catherine is practicing.

13:26.200 --> 13:27.200
Where is Aunt Nell?

13:27.200 --> 13:28.200
Nell is just not home yet.

13:28.200 --> 13:30.200
Shouldn't they deliver it?

13:30.200 --> 13:31.200
A bunch of parsley.

13:31.200 --> 13:35.200
How do you expect me to ask them to deliver a penny bunch of parsley?

13:35.200 --> 13:36.200
Oh, do not be such a lazy bone.

13:36.200 --> 13:39.200
Go down to the store and do not lose a penny.

13:39.200 --> 13:40.200
Gosh.

13:40.200 --> 13:42.200
Now what is the matter?

13:42.200 --> 13:45.200
Mama, I kind of got my stomach aching.

13:45.200 --> 13:49.200
If you do not go to the store this very minute, Dagmar Hansen,

13:49.200 --> 13:54.200
I will give you an ache someplace else.

13:54.200 --> 13:56.200
Oh, that did not hurt you.

13:56.200 --> 13:57.200
I did too.

13:57.200 --> 13:59.200
I did yapping.

13:59.200 --> 14:12.200
Oh.

14:12.200 --> 14:14.200
Time is not up yet?

14:14.200 --> 14:16.200
Just getting a drink of water, Mama.

14:16.200 --> 14:18.200
Yes.

14:18.200 --> 14:20.200
How about a smile?

14:20.200 --> 14:26.200
I'm smiling.

14:26.200 --> 14:30.200
It takes exactly 42 seconds to come from the piano, get a drink of water, and go back again.

14:30.200 --> 14:31.200
But do not worry, Mama.

14:31.200 --> 14:36.200
I'm adding it on the oven run.

14:36.200 --> 14:38.200
Oh, hello, Martha.

14:38.200 --> 14:39.200
Hello, Yanny.

14:39.200 --> 14:42.200
My feet are killing me today.

14:42.200 --> 14:43.200
Have you been shopping?

14:43.200 --> 14:44.200
No, I was just looking.

14:44.200 --> 14:47.200
I did not buy anything.

14:47.200 --> 14:50.200
This is Lynn, Chris's sister.

14:50.200 --> 14:52.200
She has been working since one o'clock.

14:52.200 --> 14:53.200
Well, foolishness.

14:53.200 --> 14:56.200
No, it's a good recipe, I can tell.

14:56.200 --> 14:58.200
Well, it should be all that work.

14:58.200 --> 15:00.200
I hope Lars likes it.

15:00.200 --> 15:04.200
Well, after five hours, he better like it.

15:04.200 --> 15:07.200
Are you coming with us to play cards tonight?

15:07.200 --> 15:08.200
No, I told you I would not.

15:08.200 --> 15:11.200
Well, I thought you might change your mind.

15:11.200 --> 15:12.200
Coffee, Yanny?

15:12.200 --> 15:13.200
I can only stay a minute.

15:13.200 --> 15:15.200
You have time for a cup of coffee.

15:15.200 --> 15:16.200
Hello, Mama.

15:16.200 --> 15:17.200
Hello, Chris.

15:17.200 --> 15:18.200
Hello, Ed, Johnny.

15:18.200 --> 15:20.200
Well, here he is.

15:20.200 --> 15:21.200
Yes, it's me.

15:21.200 --> 15:23.200
And a very good day.

15:23.200 --> 15:24.200
What's the matter?

15:24.200 --> 15:26.200
And your mama will tell you.

15:26.200 --> 15:27.200
Yes, but not now, Ed.

15:27.200 --> 15:28.200
Something bad?

15:28.200 --> 15:30.200
You will find out.

15:30.200 --> 15:32.200
Well, my conscience is clear.

15:32.200 --> 15:38.200
I'll be in my room, Mama, in case you want me.

15:38.200 --> 15:40.200
What did I tell you?

15:40.200 --> 15:42.200
The lung trouble.

15:42.200 --> 15:44.200
Oh, you're such a frog in his trough.

15:44.200 --> 15:47.200
Oh, Martha, you are always making excuses for your family.

15:47.200 --> 15:51.200
And I think today they need to be.

15:51.200 --> 15:54.200
Off into the markets to find a fat pig.

15:54.200 --> 15:57.200
All the kids, all the dead, ye giddy ye.

15:57.200 --> 15:59.200
Oh, no, Papa, ye giddy ye.

15:59.200 --> 16:01.200
Well, that's what I said, ye giddy ye.

16:01.200 --> 16:02.200
Oh, put me down a minute, Papa.

16:02.200 --> 16:03.200
I want to sell you something.

16:03.200 --> 16:05.200
Yeah, it will be a pleasure to put you down, Dagmar.

16:05.200 --> 16:07.200
You're getting heavier every minute.

16:07.200 --> 16:09.200
Oh, wait till you see what I found, Papa.

16:09.200 --> 16:10.200
What is that?

16:10.200 --> 16:11.200
A loose box.

16:11.200 --> 16:12.200
Oh, I must fix that.

16:12.200 --> 16:13.200
Oh, no, Papa.

16:13.200 --> 16:15.200
I think we're hiding away.

16:15.200 --> 16:18.200
If the board is loose, somebody might trip over it and hurt themselves, Dagmar.

16:18.200 --> 16:20.200
But you were a good girl to find it.

16:20.200 --> 16:21.200
Oh, well, bye, Papa.

16:21.200 --> 16:23.200
I'm glad you love me.

16:23.200 --> 16:24.200
Yeah.

16:24.200 --> 16:26.200
Oh, Mama.

16:26.200 --> 16:28.200
Now we go in to supper, huh?

16:28.200 --> 16:29.200
Hello, Lars.

16:29.200 --> 16:30.200
Hello, Martha.

16:30.200 --> 16:32.200
There, Dagmar.

16:32.200 --> 16:35.200
How was the tiger store at Sarah Ann's?

16:35.200 --> 16:37.200
Oh, it was all right.

16:37.200 --> 16:40.200
What is the matter with her?

16:40.200 --> 16:43.200
Well, I was cross with her today.

16:43.200 --> 16:47.200
And I had to give her a slap on the backside about so hard.

16:47.200 --> 16:48.200
Oh, only so.

16:48.200 --> 16:51.200
Yeah, well, she was not hurt, only her feelings.

16:51.200 --> 16:54.200
Well, was it a nice day, Martha?

16:54.200 --> 16:58.200
But besides that, Dagmar, Catherine did not want to practice.

16:58.200 --> 17:01.200
And Nels has been smoking again.

17:01.200 --> 17:03.200
Oh, yeah?

17:03.200 --> 17:06.200
Yeah, Jenny and I found cigarettes in his vessel.

17:06.200 --> 17:09.200
I bet it was Jenny who found them.

17:09.200 --> 17:11.200
Well, it is not easy to joke about that.

17:11.200 --> 17:13.200
He is too young to smoke.

17:13.200 --> 17:15.200
Well, what did he say?

17:15.200 --> 17:17.200
I did not talk to him.

17:17.200 --> 17:19.200
I am waiting for you to do that.

17:19.200 --> 17:21.200
Oh.

17:21.200 --> 17:24.200
Well, why should I be the one who always has to do the scolding?

17:24.200 --> 17:26.200
No, I did not say that, Martha.

17:26.200 --> 17:27.200
Well, you looked it.

17:27.200 --> 17:29.200
Well, do not worry, Martha.

17:29.200 --> 17:31.200
I will talk to him after supper, whenever that is.

17:31.200 --> 17:32.200
Oh, it is ready.

17:32.200 --> 17:34.200
Call the children.

17:34.200 --> 17:37.200
Children, supper is ready.

17:37.200 --> 17:39.200
I have to tell Dagmar to wash her hands.

17:39.200 --> 17:41.200
Wash the hands, Dagmar.

17:41.200 --> 17:43.200
I am going in now, Martha.

17:43.200 --> 17:45.200
Oh, I am hungry, Martha.

17:45.200 --> 17:47.200
Are you, now?

17:47.200 --> 17:49.200
Yeah, I could eat a horse.

17:49.200 --> 17:51.200
I could have something better than that.

17:51.200 --> 17:54.200
But, no wonder you are hungry.

17:54.200 --> 17:56.200
You have not eaten your dinner pail.

17:56.200 --> 17:58.200
There is a reason for that.

17:58.200 --> 17:59.200
Yeah?

17:59.200 --> 18:00.200
Oh, Martha?

18:00.200 --> 18:02.200
Hello, Catherine.

18:02.200 --> 18:04.200
But, Catherine, Catherine, sit down quickly.

18:04.200 --> 18:06.200
Martha is hungry.

18:06.200 --> 18:08.200
And he has not eaten his dinner pail today.

18:08.200 --> 18:09.200
And you know the reason why?

18:09.200 --> 18:10.200
Why, Papa?

18:10.200 --> 18:12.200
Well, Papa, hello, girls.

18:12.200 --> 18:14.200
Come in, Martha.

18:14.200 --> 18:15.200
It is ready.

18:15.200 --> 18:16.200
Oh, Dagmar.

18:16.200 --> 18:17.200
You do not have to shout, though.

18:17.200 --> 18:18.200
I was not shouting.

18:18.200 --> 18:19.200
Oh, you were shouting.

18:19.200 --> 18:20.200
You do it all the time.

18:20.200 --> 18:21.200
Now, be quiet.

18:21.200 --> 18:22.200
You are both at the table.

18:22.200 --> 18:23.200
Yeah, well, where is the food, Mama?

18:23.200 --> 18:26.200
It is right here.

18:26.200 --> 18:28.200
And am I ready for it?

18:28.200 --> 18:29.200
Yeah.

18:29.200 --> 18:32.200
Well, Papa did not eat his dinner pail today.

18:32.200 --> 18:33.200
And you know why?

18:33.200 --> 18:37.200
My boss, Mr. Jenkins, asked me to have dinner with him in a restaurant.

18:37.200 --> 18:38.200
Oh, really?

18:38.200 --> 18:39.200
Yeah.

18:39.200 --> 18:40.200
Papa, was the food good?

18:40.200 --> 18:41.200
Oh, it was wonderful.

18:41.200 --> 18:42.200
What did you have, Papa?

18:42.200 --> 18:46.200
Well, first of eight, I brought in the dish of branches of celery and olives.

18:46.200 --> 18:48.200
Oh, I wish we could have olives.

18:48.200 --> 18:52.200
Yeah, and then we had oxtail soup and a Parker House roll.

18:52.200 --> 18:54.200
Oh, Parker House roll.

18:54.200 --> 18:56.200
Oh, it is so good.

18:56.200 --> 19:03.200
So all through supper, Papa told us about the wonderful meal he had had that noon with Mr. Jenkins.

19:03.200 --> 19:05.200
We never went to restaurants hardly.

19:05.200 --> 19:08.200
So it was exciting to hear about one.

19:08.200 --> 19:14.200
So exciting that none of us thought to say one word about Mama's meatloaf.

19:14.200 --> 19:18.200
And then when we were all through eating, the waiter brought finger bowls.

19:18.200 --> 19:22.200
Oh, why can't we have finger bowls, Mama?

19:22.200 --> 19:26.200
Because that would mean five extra dishes to wash.

19:26.200 --> 19:29.200
But if we ate in restaurants, we would not have to wash anything.

19:29.200 --> 19:31.200
I wish we could eat in a restaurant at least once in a while, Papa.

19:31.200 --> 19:35.200
I will gladly have his folks eat in a restaurant every Thursday night, Papa.

19:35.200 --> 19:37.200
No!

19:37.200 --> 19:42.200
Martha, where are you going?

19:42.200 --> 19:43.200
Out.

19:43.200 --> 19:46.200
Oh, my God.

19:59.200 --> 20:04.200
So, there was Mrs. Edmund, walking down the street towards me.

20:04.200 --> 20:08.200
I remembered what Mrs. Johnstone had said, and I could not help giggling.

20:08.200 --> 20:10.200
Well, did you say anything to her about it?

20:10.200 --> 20:14.200
Of course she did not.

20:14.200 --> 20:16.200
Well, it is Sophie's play.

20:16.200 --> 20:17.200
Not mine.

20:17.200 --> 20:19.200
Not mine.

20:19.200 --> 20:22.200
Well, it must be you, Martha.

20:22.200 --> 20:23.200
What?

20:23.200 --> 20:27.200
Martha, for heaven's sake, keep your mind on the game.

20:27.200 --> 20:31.200
Why did you come to play cards if you're going to sit there thinking about something else?

20:31.200 --> 20:50.200
I told you I was not much of a card player.

20:50.200 --> 20:54.200
I will put down three sixes and discard the seven.

20:54.200 --> 20:58.200
Three sixes she put down.

20:58.200 --> 21:01.200
So, have you heard? Mrs. Kendall's boy is sick.

21:01.200 --> 21:02.200
The little one?

21:02.200 --> 21:03.200
Yeah, with the wrinkles.

21:03.200 --> 21:04.200
What is the matter with him?

21:04.200 --> 21:06.200
Oh, something bad.

21:06.200 --> 21:07.200
The doctors are not sure.

21:07.200 --> 21:10.200
Oh, well, if they ask me, the doctors are never sure.

21:10.200 --> 21:12.200
Oh, well, it's very strange about this.

21:12.200 --> 21:16.200
All week long, the little boy has been complaining he's sick.

21:16.200 --> 21:19.200
His head aches and his stomach aches, he says.

21:19.200 --> 21:21.200
And Mrs. Kendall, she thought he was just trying to get out of school.

21:21.200 --> 21:23.200
You know where the kids are.

21:23.200 --> 21:25.200
And now he is very, very sick.

21:25.200 --> 21:27.200
Well, let's just go up to show you.

21:27.200 --> 21:29.200
No, they are very worried about him.

21:29.200 --> 21:32.200
Whose play?

21:32.200 --> 21:36.200
Gil, it must be you again, Martha.

21:36.200 --> 21:39.200
I cannot play any longer.

21:39.200 --> 21:41.200
And why not? It is early.

21:41.200 --> 21:45.200
I just remembered something and I have to go home.

21:45.200 --> 21:46.200
Excuse me, please.

21:46.200 --> 21:48.200
But we have not had our coffee yet.

21:48.200 --> 21:50.200
Well, I take you just the same, Mrs. Lindquist.

21:50.200 --> 21:53.200
If you will excuse me.

21:53.200 --> 21:55.200
I have to go.

21:55.200 --> 22:00.200
Oh!

22:00.200 --> 22:10.200
♪♪

22:10.200 --> 22:11.200
Where did you get that from, Mama?

22:11.200 --> 22:12.200
Betty, just a second.

22:12.200 --> 22:14.200
I am just practicing again, Mama.

22:14.200 --> 22:15.200
Betty, Dagmar.

22:15.200 --> 22:16.200
Come around.

22:16.200 --> 22:17.200
Dagmar!

22:17.200 --> 22:19.200
You don't care whether I practice or not, Mama.

22:19.200 --> 22:21.200
Why, of course I do, but Dagmar!

22:21.200 --> 22:22.200
Here I am, Mama. I am being a good girl.

22:22.200 --> 22:24.200
I am getting myself ready for bed.

22:24.200 --> 22:25.200
How do you feel, Dagmar?

22:25.200 --> 22:26.200
Fine.

22:26.200 --> 22:27.200
Let me see your tongue.

22:27.200 --> 22:28.200
I feel fine, Mama.

22:28.200 --> 22:32.200
Let me see your tongue.

22:32.200 --> 22:33.200
But where is Papa?

22:33.200 --> 22:34.200
I don't know.

22:34.200 --> 22:37.200
Why is he not around at the time like this?

22:37.200 --> 22:39.200
Papa is in the cellar fixing the broken drawer.

22:39.200 --> 22:40.200
Oh.

22:40.200 --> 22:41.200
Papa, why is he in the cellar?

22:41.200 --> 22:42.200
Say, what is the matter?

22:42.200 --> 22:43.200
Nothing.

22:43.200 --> 22:45.200
Silly Mama thinks I am sick.

22:45.200 --> 22:47.200
I guess not.

22:47.200 --> 22:49.200
I guess it was my imagination.

22:49.200 --> 22:53.200
Well, I did not expect you home so soon from the Lindquist.

22:53.200 --> 22:55.200
How did you know I was at the Lindquist?

22:55.200 --> 22:58.200
Oh, Mrs. Jansrud phoned and I said you were out someplace

22:58.200 --> 23:00.200
and she said you were at the Lindquist.

23:00.200 --> 23:02.200
Mrs. Jansrud!

23:02.200 --> 23:04.200
I am glad you came home early, Mama.

23:04.200 --> 23:06.200
Now you can tuck me in.

23:06.200 --> 23:08.200
Yeah, as soon as I have taken off my coat.

23:08.200 --> 23:11.200
Oh, I thought you said I was a good girl to get myself ready for bed.

23:11.200 --> 23:13.200
Yeah, you were a good girl.

23:13.200 --> 23:16.200
And Catherine was a good girl to practice.

23:16.200 --> 23:18.200
You were both good girls.

23:18.200 --> 23:24.200
Yeah.

23:24.200 --> 23:28.200
Well, what about me to fix the drawer?

23:28.200 --> 23:31.200
It works fine now, Martha.

23:31.200 --> 23:35.200
Good.

23:35.200 --> 23:38.200
I guess I was the bad one.

23:38.200 --> 23:40.200
I had to go out without doing my dishes.

23:40.200 --> 23:42.200
The girls did them.

23:42.200 --> 23:45.200
I felt so wicked leaving them.

23:45.200 --> 23:49.200
And I was sure I was going to be punished and I was sure that mother was sick.

23:49.200 --> 23:53.200
Oh, but how could anybody else be sick much to make you feel this, Martha?

23:53.200 --> 23:57.200
Oh, that meatloaf tonight, wonderful.

23:57.200 --> 24:00.200
Not like what you could get in a restaurant.

24:00.200 --> 24:03.200
Now, where can you get food like that in a restaurant?

24:03.200 --> 24:07.200
Oh, I am glad I do not live alone like Mr. Jenkins.

24:07.200 --> 24:11.200
You know what I said to Yannick today last night?

24:11.200 --> 24:14.200
I said I wish there was an old maid.

24:14.200 --> 24:17.200
A fine old maid you would make, Martha.

24:17.200 --> 24:19.200
No, but I did not mean it.

24:19.200 --> 24:29.200
I would not have things any different than what they are ever.

24:29.200 --> 24:31.200
Now I go tuck Dagmar in.

24:31.200 --> 24:32.200
Do not worry, Martha.

24:32.200 --> 24:33.200
She is not sick.

24:33.200 --> 24:36.200
I know.

24:36.200 --> 24:40.200
Last night you speak to Nels about, you know what?

24:40.200 --> 24:45.200
Well, I have been fixing the drawer and everything. I did not have time.

24:45.200 --> 24:46.200
Oh, heaven's sakes, Martha.

24:46.200 --> 24:53.200
Do I have to do everything around here?

24:53.200 --> 24:55.200
No.

24:55.200 --> 24:59.200
Papa did not have to do everything in our house.

24:59.200 --> 25:03.200
I remember by the next morning, mama's bad day was forgotten.

25:03.200 --> 25:08.200
That was when we had a visit from one of our favorite people on Steiner Street.

25:08.200 --> 25:15.200
Mr. Staley, the parcel postman, who brought the mail to our house when Mr. Larkson's feet came out.

25:15.200 --> 25:28.200
On cold days, I remember, when mama heard his ring, she would invite him in to rest for a few minutes and warm his hands and have a good hot cup of coffee.

25:28.200 --> 25:38.200
Dagmar liked to have him stop in too, because he usually brought her stamps from foreign lands for her stamp album.

25:38.200 --> 25:43.200
How Mr. Staley enjoyed those few moments of mama's hospitality.

25:43.200 --> 25:53.200
And when that hospitality includes a cheering cup of Maxwell House, coffee time becomes one of the happiest moments of the day.

25:53.200 --> 26:02.200
Of course, enjoying Maxwell House is an old family custom in this country, and the reasons might be plain to see in the faces of coffee lovers everywhere.

26:02.200 --> 26:12.200
It's flavor, wonderful, heartwarming flavor, that comes from our own recipe, the one and only recipe for good to the last drop flavor.

26:12.200 --> 26:19.200
You see, down in the coffee countries to the south, coffee grows in scores of different varieties, no two alike in flavor.

26:19.200 --> 26:26.200
And there are more ways to put those coffees together in a blend than there are stamps in Dagmar's album.

26:26.200 --> 26:33.200
But the whole world over, there's only one way, one recipe for that famous Maxwell House flavor.

26:33.200 --> 26:44.200
Yes, it's the world famous recipe that makes Maxwell House such a family favorite, that year after year keeps folks passing their cups and saying,

26:44.200 --> 26:49.200
best tasting coffee I've had in a month of Sundays.

26:49.200 --> 26:53.200
Another way of putting it, of course, is the sign of good coffee.

26:53.200 --> 26:59.200
Everywhere you see it, you know it means fragrant, good to the last drop Maxwell House.

26:59.200 --> 27:03.200
Make a point to buy it in the blue tin tomorrow, won't you?

27:03.200 --> 27:32.200
Always good to the last drop.

